What is Java?
    Java is a high-level, object-oriented programming language. It was created by James Gosling in 1995 at Sun Microsystems. It was originally called OAK in 1991.


Key Features:
    Simple:
        Java is easy to learn if you know basic programming. It removes complex things like pointers.

    Secure:
        It is safe because programs run inside a "protected area" called the JVM. It doesn't let programs touch sensitive parts of your computer easily.

    Object-Oriented:
        Everything in Java is treated as an "Object." This helps keep code organized.

    Robust:
        Java is strong because it manages memory for you. A Garbage Collector automatically cleans up unused memory so the programmer doesn't have to.

    Portable:
        You can run Java code on any machine that has a JVM installed.

    Architecture Neutral:
        Java follows the rule: "Write once, run anywhere." It works the same way even if you upgrade your computer hardware.

    Distributed:
        Java is built for the internet. It can handle networking (TCP/IP) easily, allowing different computers to work together.

Java Program Compilation:
    Java program compiler converts the java program file into a highly optimized class file called bytecode.


How Java Works?
    Compilation: A compiler takes your code and turns it into Bytecode (a .class file).

    Execution:
        Class Loader:
            Brings the class file into the JVM.
        Bytecode Verifier:
            Checks the code to make sure it is safe and doesn't break any rules.
        Interpreter:
            Reads the bytecode and runs it line-by-line.


JDK, JRE, and JVM:
    JDK (Java Development Kit):
        The full toolbox. It has everything you need to write and run Java programs (Compiler, Debugger, etc.).

    JRE (Java Runtime Environment):
        The mini-toolbox. It has the minimum tools needed to run an existing Java app.

    JVM (Java Virtual Machine):
        The engine. It is the part that actually runs the code and talks to your computer's hardware. It starts by looking for the main() method in your program.
